  "releaseNotes": "v next\nBREAKING CHANGES\n- The Node entry script is hand-written CommonJS (entrypoint-http.cjs) instead of a webpack bundle\nof TypeScript 3 sources. The package no longer ships or builds any npm toolchain: Yarn.MSBuild,\nwebpack and the package's own node_modules are gone. Modules are loaded with import(), so .mjs and\nES-module boot files work, and the `new Function` workaround is gone.\n- The RPC channel to Node uses System.Text.Json instead of Newtonsoft.Json, with camelCase property\nnames and dictionary keys as before. The package no longer depends on Newtonsoft.Json. Invocation\narguments and results are serialized with the reflection-based resolver: apps that publish trimmed\nor native-AOT must keep the types they exchange with Node.\n- StringAsTempFile is now internal. It was an implementation detail of how the entry script reaches\nNode.\n- OutOfProcessNodeInstance's constructor is now protected and takes (entryPointScript,\ncommandLineArguments, NodeServicesOptions) instead of eleven parameters. Every other value was\nalready a NodeServicesOptions property. The finalizer is gone: an undisposed instance's node ends\nwith the .NET process (job object on Windows, parent-PID poll elsewhere).\n- Nullable annotations: exportedFunctionName / exportNameOrNull and NodeInvocationException's\ndetails are string?; NodeInvocationInfo.ExportedFunctionName is string?.\n- Calling INodeServices after it was disposed throws ObjectDisposedException instead of starting a\nnew node process.\n- Changes below node_modules, .angular, .git, .vs, bin and obj (relative to ProjectPath) no longer\nrestart Node. The Angular CLI's cache in .angular/cache rewrites .mjs/.js/.json files while\nng serve or ng build --watch runs, which caused needless restarts.\nFIXES\n- Node processes no longer outlive their host:\n- On Windows, node joins the same kill-on-close job object as the npm and Angular CLI processes,\nso it ends with .NET even when the debugger is stopped or the process is killed.\n- Disposing INodeServices also ends instances still in their 15-second draining period.\n- A constructor that failed after node had started (e.g. a ProjectPath that does not exist)\nleft that node running; it is now killed.\n- A failing kill no longer escapes Dispose and leaves the process handle undisposed.\n- Invocation arguments are serialized asynchronously, so an IAsyncEnumerable - such as an Entity\nFramework IQueryable handed to SupplyData as it is - is enumerated, as Newtonsoft did, instead of\nfailing with \"can only be serialized using async serialization methods\".\n- dotnet publish (and a Debug build without node_modules) failed with \"The working directory\n\"ClientApp/\" does not exist\" when installed from NuGet. The relative SpaRoot was passed to\nnpm-install.proj, which resolved it against the package folder; it is now made absolute against\nthe consuming project first.\nFEATURES\n- .mjs and .cjs are now watched by default, alongside .js, .ts, .json and .html, so a rebuilt\nES-module bundle restarts Node. ES modules cannot be evicted from Node's cache, so the restart\nis what loads the new code.\n- PublishNodeModules (MSBuild, default: the value of BuildServerSideRenderer) controls whether\nnode_modules is copied into the publish output. The Angular application builder bundles server\ndependencies into dist/server, so such apps can set it to false.\nv 10.4.1\nFIXES\n- EmbeddedResourceReader now names the resource and the assembly it could not find. A missing\nresource previously surfaced as ArgumentNullException (Parameter 'stream'), which identified\nneither.",
